# Consolidated Red-Team Disposition

## Verdict

The original draft was **blocked**. Four independent reviewers found real
authority, cutover, rollback, authorization, scale, and integration-path gaps.
The plan has been revised. It is now suitable for product/finance review, but
implementation remains blocked until the decision gates in Phase 1 are
accepted.

## Consolidated Findings

| ID | Severity | Finding | Disposition |
|---|---|---|---|
| RT-01 | Critical | No existing role satisfied the named CUS cost-check workflow. | Resolved: CUS maps to `CLERK` with a narrow, row-scoped recoverable-cost read/request resource; no coarse finance access or approval rights. |
| RT-02 | High | Phase 2 communication work was a false hard dependency for Debit Note generation. | Resolved: Phases 2 and 3 may run in parallel after Phase 1; Phase 3 consumes stable existing shipment/customer/legal-entity IDs. |
| RT-03 | Critical | Legal-invoice authority was presented as a choice while the architecture assumed reference-only AR. | Decision gate: finance/legal must accept reference-only or approve a complete one-posting replacement/alias design before provider work. |
| RT-04 | Critical | Legal-invoice outbox lacked crash-safe recovery and duplicated the repo's durable-effect authority. | Resolved: extend the existing durable-effect dispatcher, leases, dedupe, status probe, compare-and-set result, and dead-letter remediation. |
| RT-05 | High | Phase 4 hid a full treasury product inside AR/AP integration. | Resolved: Phase 4 now contains only minimal account/movement/book-balance foundation; statement import, matching, transfers, close/reopen, manual adjustment, and full workspace are deferred to a separate approved plan. |
| RT-06 | Critical | Treasury cutover could strand pending approvals, break old idempotency keys, or double-count opening balance/history. | Contract added: versioned payment snapshots, pending-action disposition, immutable per-account cutover watermark, physical transaction identity, and explicit balance boundary. Acceptance remains a Phase 1 gate. |
| RT-07 | Critical | Rollback could restore a build that posts ledger money without treasury linkage. | Resolved: post-cutover compatibility floor gates create/check/approve/refund/reversal as one unit and requires early rejection before ledger mutation. |
| RT-08 | High | Authorization consumers could drift: CLERK row scope, cached capability booleans, global search, and executive endpoint reach. | Resolved: live per-request row scope, policy-derived/versioned capability envelope, search derived from the same catalog, and explicit `ADMIN`/`MANAGER` executive API guards. |
| RT-09 | High | Customer interaction timeline had no safe ownership or portal-visibility contract. | Decision/contract added: separate milestone, delivery, immutable customer-visible content, and acknowledgement authorities with allowlisted portal DTOs. |
| RT-10 | Medium | Customer dispute had no required reason, version, or idempotency evidence. | Resolved: bounded reason, mandatory key/version, immutable dispute event, scoped actor, and concurrent confirm/dispute tests. |
| RT-11 | High | Treasury admin/governance and physical payment identity were underspecified. | Resolved for the minimal foundation: separate read/operate/admin authority, registered governance subjects, and account-scoped physical transaction uniqueness. |
| RT-12 | High | Scale contracts missed one receipt over 200 obligations and allowed full in-memory AR/AP scans behind paginated UI. | Resolved: one gross receipt with bounded/resumable allocation or explicit unapplied remainder; database bounds precede hydration with query-plan/latency/memory evidence. |
| RT-13 | High | Profitability snapshot had no canonical financial version or owner on the real trip-close path. | Resolved: shared financial posting/version ID; capture in the real `trip-status-machine` transaction; governed correction/reopen supersession through `adjustment-governance`. |
| RT-14 | High | Dashboard `asOf` could combine AR, treasury, and cached P&L from different committed states. | Resolved: one repeatable-read transaction or watermark-fence/retry protocol; mixed snapshots cannot be labeled reconciled. |
| RT-15 | High | Dispatcher and salesperson attribution lacked production-grade owning workflows. | Contract added: authenticated accepted-dispatcher event and versioned salesperson assignment with effective dates/reassignment. Historical gaps remain explicit; acceptance is a Phase 1 gate. |

