# Accounting and Plan Dependencies

## Scope Note

Current-task authority from the user:

`Booking -> Customer Service -> Điều vận -> Phát sinh chi phí -> CUS kiểm tra chi phí -> Lập Debit Note -> Kế toán duyệt -> Xuất hóa đơn -> Theo dõi công nợ -> Thu tiền -> Báo cáo lợi nhuận`

The workflow map below is aligned to that sequence. Repo sources are still used for the behavioral rules, state transitions, and dependency order, but this user-supplied chain is the current-task authority for end-to-end flow ordering.

Sources consulted: `docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md`, `ROADMAP.md`, `docs/regression-testing/02-module-02-pricing-revenue.md`, `03-module-03-cus.md`, `04-module-04-disbursement-recovery.md`, `05-module-05-ar.md`, `06-module-06-ap.md`, `11-module-11-finance-pnl.md`, `plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-02-wave-1-pricing-fuel-data-layer.md`, `phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md`, `phase-04-wave-3-financial-close.md`, `phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md`, `plans/260727-1230-approved-business-rules/plan.md`, `plans/260729-1908-shipment-operations-workspace/plan.md`, `plans/260729-1248-customer-portal-overhaul/plan.md`, `plans/260726-2145-customer-portal-repair/plan.md`, `shared/src/calculations/round.ts`, `shared/src/calculations/tripTotals.ts`.

## 1. Workflow / State-Transition Map

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Shipment draft] --> B[Docs verified]
  B --> C[Dispatch / trip created]
  C --> D[Trip completed]
  D --> E[Approved expenses]
  E --> F[Debit note drafted]
  F --> G[Debit note issued / sent]
  G --> H[Customer confirm / dispute]
  H --> I[Payment recorded]
  I --> J[AR allocated]
  I --> K[AR unallocated / overpayment]
  E --> L[Supplier invoice / AP path]
  L --> M[Fuel AP reconciliation]
  M --> N[Manual offset request]
  N --> O[Approved bilateral offset]
  O --> P[AP/AR reduced, audit kept]
  J --> Q[Profitability / dashboard]
  M --> Q
  C --> Q
  D --> Q
```

### Layered states

- Shipment: `DRAFT -> IN_PROGRESS -> DELIVERED -> CLOSED`, with `CANCELED` terminal. Wave 0 already implemented this shipment backbone, and Wave 2 explicitly builds CUS on top of it (ROADMAP.md:82-88, ROADMAP.md:120-289; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:21-33).
- Expense verification: `PENDING -> APPROVED` or `REJECTED`; invoice-required items must have full invoice data before approval, no-invoice items only for allowed categories with substitute evidence, and over-threshold items route to approval instead of silent acceptance (docs/regression-testing/04-module-04-disbursement-recovery.md:411-560; ROADMAP.md:498-501).
- Debit note: `DRAFT -> SENT -> PENDING_CONFIRM -> CONFIRMED -> PARTIAL_PAID -> PAID`, with `REJECTED` and `CANCELED` terminal; confirmed notes lock and later changes move to adjustment docs only (docs/regression-testing/03-module-03-cus.md:447-520; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:59-75).
- AR allocation: payment with instruction allocates per instruction; if no instruction, oldest-due-date first; overpayment stays unallocated, not negative AR (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:54-61; docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:384-453).
- AP offset: same-entity + same-currency manual offset only, capped at the smaller side, with approval and reversal on cancel (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:103-110; docs/regression-testing/06-module-06-ap.md:245-323).
- Period/lock: salary and fuel lock monthly; debit-note lock follows customer payment cycle, default monthly and weekly only if contract says so; late data becomes adjustment in the open period linked to origin period (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:237-244; ROADMAP.md:737-740).

## 2. Accepted vs Pending / Unverified

### Accepted, authoritative rules

All Q01-Q23 in the Q&A doc are accepted as written and are now formal requirements, not proposals (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:36-270; plans/260727-1230-approved-business-rules/plan.md:27-30, 90-109; ROADMAP.md:24-26, 37-40).

Most material accepted rules for this workflow:

| Area | Accepted rule | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| AR credit control | Q01: warn at 80%, hard stop at 100%, configurable per customer | Drives customer limit UX and shipment gating (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:36-43; docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:172-217). |
| AR approvals | Q02: CUS/dispatch cannot self-approve over-limit service continuation; Finance Manager/Director approval tiered by percent and cap | Shapes authority boundary and override UI (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:45-52; docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:219-245). |
| Payment allocation | Q03: instruction first, otherwise oldest-due-date first; overpayment stays unallocated | Core to cash application and debits (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:54-61; docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:384-453). |
| Reminder policy | Q04/Q05: business-hours schedule, holiday roll, 1/day cap; customer email primary, in-app backup, 3 retries | Affects scheduler, notification, and portal behavior (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:63-79; docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:457-520). |
| AP fuel | Q06: one fuel invoice may cover many trucks; allocation by actual slips/logs, not even split | Necessary for fuel AP reconciliation and supplier invoice entry (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:85-92; docs/regression-testing/06-module-06-ap.md:245-323). |
| Supplier taxonomy | Q07: one supplier can have multiple categories, one primary category for reporting | Prevents category drift in AP and dashboard rollups (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:94-101; docs/regression-testing/06-module-06-ap.md:94-153). |
| Partner duality | Q08: one partner profile by tax code, AR and AP stay separate, offsets manual only | Critical for cross-ledger correctness and legal-entity scoping (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:103-110; docs/regression-testing/00-cross-cutting.md:271-280; docs/regression-testing/06-module-06-ap.md:245-323). |
| Payroll close | Q09-Q11: company-period close, block whole period on money-affecting error, adjust preferred after close, reopen only before issue/payment/posting | Governs period lock and reversal flow (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:116-143; ROADMAP.md:532-559). |
| No-invoice disbursements | Q12-Q14: allowed categories, substitute evidence, 1M/item and 5M/person/day defaults, missing evidence returns, thresholded approval | Controls what reaches debit notes and what gets approved (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:148-174; docs/regression-testing/04-module-04-disbursement-recovery.md:487-560). |
| Roles and scope | Q15-Q18: maker/checker/approver split, 1 legal entity per CUSTOMER account by default, CLERK can edit only shipment-doc surfaces pre-dispatch, locked data changes via adjustment only | Sets the permission model for CUS and finance (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:179-214; ROADMAP.md:721-728, 700-705). |
| Date / period / source-of-truth | Q19-Q23: next-business-day roll, completion-date accounting for trip revenue/salary/count/P&L, monthly salary/fuel lock, debit-note cycle by customer, recompute pre-lock and versioned adjustment post-lock, idempotent writes and first-approve-wins | This is the backbone of all period logic and conflict handling (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:219-263; ROADMAP.md:732-740, 737-740). |

### Pending / unverified items that still materially affect implementation

These are the unresolved items that still show up in roadmap phase files or module docs and can change the implementation shape:

| Item | Why it is still open | Impact |
|---|---|---|
| M3.1 mandatory shipment fields at create vs before dispatch | Wave 2 still lists it as an open PRD question | Affects shipment form schema and validation (ROADMAP.md:430-440; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:171-185). |
| M3.3 two-way messaging in v1 | Wave 2 still asks whether messaging is in v1 or notifications+ack only | Changes portal scope and notification model (ROADMAP.md:430-438; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:98-109, 173-176). |
| M3.4 free-time rules table | Free-time table is still requested explicitly | Affects delivery-confirmation and fee calculation UI/data model (ROADMAP.md:430-438; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:52-58, 177-178). |
| M11.2 truck-specific vs shared cost allocation | Wave 4 still asks for exact cost-allocation policy | Directly affects per-truck P&L and the unallocated bucket (ROADMAP.md:708-710; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:67-76, 148-150). |
| M11.4 payment-term evaluation granularity | Wave 4 asks whether it is per-invoice or averaged per customer | Changes how the dashboard interprets paid portions (ROADMAP.md:708-710; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:70-72, 165-166). |
| M11.5 exact KPI list | Wave 4 still needs KPI definitions | Affects dashboard cards, drilldowns, and labels (ROADMAP.md:708-710; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:73-76, 166-167). |
| M12.2 credit-note same-period netting | Still explicitly open | Affects monthly fuel reconciliation and period-close assumptions (ROADMAP.md:711; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:78-83, 168). |

## 3. Business Definitions

### Revenue

Transport revenue is not a handwritten free-form number anymore. The pricing wave defines auto-revenue as `weight × tier.pricePerKg + surcharges + VAT`, with pre-lock recompute and post-lock adjustment only (ROADMAP.md:298-324; docs/regression-testing/02-module-02-pricing-revenue.md:84-107). The shared calculation authority then splits the number into `freightExVat` and `recordedRevenue`, where `recordedRevenue = freightExVat - customerCommission` (shared/src/calculations/tripTotals.ts:140-167, 191-207).

Ancillary revenue is separate from transport P&L. The pricing roadmap treats it as its own table and says refunds use negative amounts with reason, no silent edits (plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-02-wave-1-pricing-fuel-data-layer.md:56-60, 114-118; docs/regression-testing/02-module-02-pricing-revenue.md:139-165).

### Cost

For owned trucks, total trip cost is fuel + road allowance + tolls + salary + bonuses + shifts; for external trips, cost is the external freight cost only. The shared calculator keeps toll cost separate from road allowance so the P&L can show the full picture without mixing categories (shared/src/calculations/tripTotals.ts:118-189). For AP/disbursement work, approved expenses are the pass-through cost layer that later feeds debit notes and AR; unapproved disbursements stay out of the official document (plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:59-67; docs/regression-testing/04-module-04-disbursement-recovery.md:330-381, 487-560).

### Profit

Gross profit is `recordedRevenue - totalCost` for owned trips, while external trips use `recordedRevenue - externalFreightCost` as the management margin. The code deliberately keeps `serviceMargin` out of the transport P&L and tracks `externalMargin` separately (shared/src/calculations/tripTotals.ts:169-207). On the reporting side, the dashboard and per-truck P&L must expose truck-specific costs, shared costs in an unallocated bucket, and still list trucks with costs but no trips (docs/regression-testing/11-module-11-finance-pnl.md:104-180, 350-426; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:65-76, 122-140).

### Pass-through

Pass-through is the recoverable layer: approved disbursements, freight billed to the customer, and similar recoverable items that move through CUS into debit notes and AR. The key rule is that only approved disbursements enter the correct period, and a disbursement cannot appear on two debit notes (docs/regression-testing/04-module-04-disbursement-recovery.md:330-381; ROADMAP.md:419-449; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:59-67, 84-88).

### AR

AR is the customer-side receivable ledger. The AR module requires explicit separation of freight, disbursement, and other groups so totals reconcile, and it requires total AR reporting with opening, activity, receipts, adjustments, and closing balances (docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:249-336, 319-380). Payment allocation follows instruction-first and oldest-due-date fallback, with overpayment held as unallocated cash, not negative AR (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:54-61; docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:384-453).

### AP

AP is the supplier-side payable ledger. Fuel AP can cover many trucks on one invoice, supplier categories can be multi-valued, and bilateral debt offset is manual only, with approval and a hard cap at the smaller side of AR/AP (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:85-110; docs/regression-testing/06-module-06-ap.md:245-323; ROADMAP.md:485-495). That means AP is not a mirror image of AR: it has its own invoice/taxation and reconciliation rules.

### Invoice timing and period rules

- Debit notes can be generated by shipment or by period; default is monthly, weekly only if the contract says so (plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:59-67; docs/regression-testing/03-module-03-cus.md:366-445).
- Payment/processing dates that fall on weekend/holiday roll to the next business day, but the original date is still shown and contract-calendar override wins if specified (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:219-226).
- Trip revenue, salary, trip count, and P&L use the completion-date period; attendance, fuel, and expenses use actual event date (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:228-235).
- Salary and fuel lock monthly; debit-note lock follows the customer payment cycle, default monthly and weekly only if the contract says so (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:237-244).
- Late data after lock becomes an adjustment in the open period linked back to the origin period; do not overwrite the closed record in place (docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:246-253; docs/regression-testing/11-module-11-finance-pnl.md:317-347).

## 4. Minimum Viable Scope vs Deferred

### Must ship together

Ranked by dependency and blast radius:

1. CUS shipment handling and verification. The workflow starts with Wave 0 shipment authority and Wave 2 CUS workflow; without that, the rest of the chain has nothing authoritative to point at (ROADMAP.md:82-88, 289-295, 383-449; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:13-17, 21-33).
2. Expense verification and disbursement classification. This is the bridge from field spend to billable recovery, and it gates what can enter a debit note (docs/regression-testing/04-module-04-disbursement-recovery.md:411-560; ROADMAP.md:498-501).
3. Debit note assembly and locking. Without this, AR has no stable source document, and portal confirm/dispute flows remain undefined (docs/regression-testing/03-module-03-cus.md:366-520; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:59-75).
4. AR allocation, reminders, and overpayment handling. These are the customer-cash controls that close the loop after debit note issue (docs/regression-testing/05-module-05-ar.md:172-520).
5. AP fuel reconciliation and manual offset. This is the supplier-side mirror, but it must remain manual/approved, never auto-offset (docs/regression-testing/06-module-06-ap.md:245-323; docs/prd/business-logic-qa-proposals.md:103-110).
6. Profitability and dashboard feed. Per-truck P&L, payment-term evaluation, and director dashboard are downstream consumers of the closed-loop data, not standalone visuals (docs/regression-testing/11-module-11-finance-pnl.md:104-430; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:65-84).

### Safe to defer if scope must be trimmed

- Two-way messaging in the customer portal, because the roadmap still treats it as an open v1 question (ROADMAP.md:435-437; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:98-99, 173-176).
- Exact M11.5 KPI definitions, because the reporting surface can be built with placeholders only if the user accepts a narrower first release (ROADMAP.md:708-710; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:73-76).
- Credit-note same-period netting, because it affects monthly fuel reconciliation but not the core AR/AP workflow (ROADMAP.md:711; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:78-83, 168).
- Fine-grained free-time tables and portal live-location display, because they improve UX but do not change the accounting chain itself (ROADMAP.md:435-438; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:52-58, 176-178).

## 5. Recommended Plan Dependencies

### Recommended `blockedBy`

| Target dependency | Recommendation | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| `plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/plan.md` | `blockedBy` | It defines the only accepted wave order and still lists open questions by wave; this workflow sits across Waves 2-4 (ROADMAP.md:94-101, 289-295, 430-449, 506-546, 694-711, 715-740). |
| `plans/260727-1230-approved-business-rules/plan.md` | `blockedBy` | It is the completed authority baseline for Q01-Q23 and explicitly says later phases depend on that baseline (plans/260727-1230-approved-business-rules/plan.md:27-37, 75-93). |
| `plans/260729-1908-shipment-operations-workspace/plan.md` | `blockedBy` for any CLERK/shipment-edit slice | It is the completed shipment/dispatch workspace that defines the CLERK editable shipment surface and preserves shipment authority and audit contracts (plans/260729-1908-shipment-operations-workspace/plan.md:30-55). |
| `plans/260729-1248-customer-portal-overhaul/plan.md` and `plans/260726-2145-customer-portal-repair/plan.md` | not hard blockers, but required overlap references | They already define the customer portal record surface and scope. Reuse them instead of re-inventing portal behavior (plans/260729-1248-customer-portal-overhaul/plan.md:21-45; plans/260726-2145-customer-portal-repair/plan.md:5-29). |

### Recommended `blocks`

For the eventual implementation plan, the only honest `blocks` are downstream phase items in this same workflow family:

- Wave 2 CUS/portal items remain blocked until shipment authority, document verification, and debit-note assembly rules are pinned. That is already the structure in the roadmap and phase file (ROADMAP.md:383-449; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-03-wave-2-cus-core-customer-portal.md:36-90).
- Wave 3 financial-close items remain blocked until AR/AP allocation, fuel AP reconciliation, and period-lock rules are fixed by Q01-Q23 and the wave docs (ROADMAP.md:455-559; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-04-wave-3-financial-close.md:34-99).
- Wave 4 reporting items remain blocked until the cost-allocation policy, KPI definitions, and credit-note behavior are confirmed (ROADMAP.md:563-711; plans/silversea-prd-roadmap/phase-05-wave-4-mobile-director-reporting.md:32-83, 157-168).

## 6. Bottom Line

The accounting chain is coherent if, and only if, the system treats it as one linked authority path:

`shipment -> trip -> approved expense -> debit note -> AR/AP -> profitability/dashboard`

The non-negotiable rules are already accepted in Q01-Q23. The remaining material risks are not the money rules themselves, but the few open module-specific questions around shipment creation, portal messaging, free-time, KPI definitions, and cost allocation. Those are the only items that should still be allowed to block implementation decisions.

## 7. Unresolved Business Decisions That Still Matter

1. M3.1 mandatory shipment fields at create vs before dispatch.
2. M3.3 whether two-way messaging is in v1.
3. M3.4 the free-time rules table.
4. M11.2 exact truck-specific vs shared cost policy.
5. M11.4 payment-term evaluation granularity.
6. M11.5 exact KPI definitions.
7. M12.2 credit-note same-period netting.

These are the smallest remaining decision points that can still materially change schema, UI, or reporting logic.
