## Executive Summary
Silversea already has the right structural pieces for a CUS-to-finance lifecycle: a shared page catalog, role-gated router, a custom office shell, a separate customer portal shell, and dense list pages that switch between desktop tables and mobile card stacks. The problem is not lack of screens. The problem is that the route gates and the visible navigation are out of sync on several high-value finance and dispatch pages, so the workflow is fragmented by role rather than by task.

The main recommendation is to keep the existing top-level routes and shells, but recompose them into one task-oriented lifecycle: shipment intake and detail, dispatch handoff, cost review, debit note, AR collection, AP/fuel, and profitability. Do not create a parallel design system or a second app shell. Instead, use the current shell plus role-specific sections, sticky filters, bounded tables, and detail sidebars/slideovers so the app stays usable on desktop, tablet, and mobile without long-page overflow.

The biggest gap is authorization UX, not visual styling. `ACCOUNTANT`, `MANAGER`, and `ADMIN` currently see finance and dispatch items in the sidebar, but several of those routes are blocked by `adminOnly` or `managerOrAdminOnly` guards. `CLERK` has create/docs surfaces but no obvious shipment queue surface. `CEO` is not a distinct role in the app model; treat it as a business title mapped onto existing `MANAGER`/`ADMIN` policy.

