- Tailkit: `a-l-dark-sidebar-06`, `a-c-navigation-08`, `a-c-page-headings-04`, `a-c-page-headings-03`, `a-c-statistics-11`, `a-c-statistics-12`, `a-c-tables-14`, `a-c-tables-15`.
- Untitled UI: `sidebar-dual-tier`, `filter-bar`, `breadcrumbs`, `table`, `empty-state`, `charts-base`, `command-menu`.
- Shadcn: no direct MCP surface was exposed in this session, so I did not cite live component IDs for it. I used the repo's own minimal primitives as the Shadcn-like baseline instead.

## Current Inventory

### Route and shell layer
- Shared route authority: `PAGE_CATALOG` and `routes` are the single path/title source.
- Office shell: `Layout` with role-based side nav, desktop sidebar, topbar, profile/password menus, and bottom-nav only for driver portal.
- Customer shell: `CustomerPortalLayout` with mobile drawer, desktop sidebar, legal-entity scope bar, and bottom nav.
- Router guards: `App.tsx` currently hard-codes `customerOnly`, `clerkOrAdminOnly`, `shipmentOperatorOnly`, `officeStaffOnly`, `managerOrAdminOnly`, `adminOnly`, and `strictAdminOnly`.

### Page inventory by lifecycle step
- Shipment intake: `ShipmentsPage`, `ShipmentDetailPage`, `ClerkShipmentCreatePage`, `ClerkShipmentDocsPage`.
- Dispatch: `DispatchPage`, `TripListPage`, `TripDetailPage`, `TripCreatePage`, `TripEditPage`, `ForwarderTripsPage`, `ForwarderTripDetailPage`.
- Cost review / expenses: `ExpenseListPage`, `ExpenseEntryPage`, `payables-fuel-invoices` surface inside `PayableListPage`.
- Debit note / AR: `DebtListPage`, `DebtDetailPage`, `PortalDebitNotesPage`, `PortalStatementPage`.
- AP: `PayableListPage`, `PayableDetailPage`, `SupplierListPage`.
- Profitability: `ProfitPage`, `FinancePage`.
- Dashboard and governance: `DashboardPage`, `CreditOverrideQueuePage`, `GovernanceActionsPage`, `AuditLogPage`, `UsersPage`.

### Test and CSS coverage already in place
- `ShipmentsPage.test.tsx` covers create-button gating, mobile pagination, stale-response handling, and 44px filter controls.
- `DispatchPage.test.tsx` covers dispatch queue behavior, reassignment, and role-aware fake data wiring.
- `PortalPages.test.tsx` covers row-scoped portal APIs, customer-scope switching, statement export, and mobile portal pagination behavior.
- `ShipmentsPage.css`, `DispatchPage.css`, and `CustomerPortalLayout.css` already implement bounded desktop/mobile layouts instead of one endless document.

## Role Matrix

| Role | Current visible shell | Current route reality | Workflow fit | Main gap |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `CLERK` | Office shell, plus clerk quick-create/docs routes | Can reach `/clerk/shipments/new` and `/clerk/shipments/:id/docs`; cannot see `/shipments` list/detail today | Good for intake entry and doc completion | No clerk shipment queue/hub to review the working set |
| `DISPATCH` | Office shell | `DispatchPage` route exists, but it is guarded by `managerOrAdminOnly` | Good for manager/admin dispatching, not read-only dispatch review | Accountant sees dispatch nav but is blocked by route guard |
| `ACCOUNTANT` | Office shell | Sidebar shows finance items, but `/dispatch`, `/profit`, `/debt`, and `/payables` are blocked or admin-only | Strong fit for AR/AP/collections/governance | Nav and guard mismatch; finance surfaces are inconsistent |
| `MANAGER` | Office shell | Has dispatch, shipments, governance, users, audit, some config; cannot reach admin-only finance pages | Good for operation control and approvals | Profit/AR/AP pages should either open or disappear from nav |
| `ADMIN` | Office shell | Full coverage across all office routes | Full operational superuser fit | No functional gap, only UX density risk |
| `CEO` | Not a distinct role enum | No `Role.CEO` in current app model; business title maps onto existing admin/manager policy | Treat as decision maker / director approval layer | Needs policy mapping, not a new role constant |
| `CUSTOMER` | Customer portal shell | `/portal/shipments`, `/portal/debit-notes`, `/portal/statement` only | Good for track + confirm + collect | Portal is separate from office shell by design; keep it that way |

## Information Architecture Recommendation

### Recommended hierarchy
1. Keep `/shipments` as the master intake/detail entry point for office users.
2. Keep `/dispatch` as the execution workspace for live trip movement and queue management.
3. Keep `/debt`, `/payables`, and `/profit` as finance workspaces, not as dashboard widgets only.
4. Keep `/portal/*` as the customer-only track/confirm/statement space.
5. Keep `/clerk/*` as the document-entry sub-workspace when the clerk needs a tighter queue than the general shipment list.

### Proposed task flow
`Shipment intake -> shipment detail -> dispatch handoff -> cost review -> debit note -> AR collection -> AP/fuel -> profitability`

### Proposed route behavior
- `/shipments`: master list, search, filter, status, and create for authorized roles.
- `/shipments/:id`: the operational hub for CUS detail, documents, version history, dispatch readiness, and financial handoff markers.
- `/debt/:id`: AR detail hub with aging, collections, payment entry, debit-note linkage, and customer statement context.
- `/payables/:id`: supplier/AP detail hub with fuel, ancillary, and outsourced cost breakdown.
- `/profit`: monthly/quarterly profitability view, not a hidden admin-only report.
- `/portal/*`: customer-facing mirror of shipment, debit note, and statement.

### What not to do
- Do not create a second app shell for finance.
- Do not add a new parallel design system.
- Do not bury role-specific actions in long pages with no fixed control rail.

## DesktopMobile Layout Rules

### Desktop
- Use a bounded left shell and a main workspace, not a vertical page that grows forever.
- Put the work queue on the left and the detail/approval panel on the right for dense flows.
- Keep tables inside scroll containers with fixed column widths for long identifiers and dates.
- Make filters sticky or rail-based so the queue does not lose control context.

### Tablet
- Collapse side panels into tabs or a two-state toggle once horizontal space drops below the safe threshold.
- Keep summary cards visible at the top; move low-priority details into accordions.
- Preserve one primary action per screen state.

### Mobile
- Switch from table-first to card-first list rendering.
- Use 44px touch targets, visible labels, and one-step confirm actions.
- Keep the primary action visible at the bottom or in a sticky footer, not off-screen.
- Never rely on horizontal scrolling as the only way to reach core actions.

### Accessibility and state
- All icon-only controls need `aria-label`.
- Active filters need `aria-pressed` or an equivalent state announcement.
- Loading and empty states need in-page messaging, not just skeletons.
- Menu close/open should restore focus to the trigger.
- Role-gated actions should not be visible as dead links.
- Debits, collections, debit-note confirmation, and approval actions need explicit state feedback and visible audit trail cues.

## Library Pattern References

### Tailkit, ranked best fit
1. `a-l-dark-sidebar-06` for a compact office shell with always-visible brand rail plus full sidebar.
2. `a-c-navigation-08` for role-filtered sidebar groups and nested navigation state.
3. `a-c-page-headings-04` for nested finance/detail pages with breadcrumb + actions.
4. `a-c-page-headings-03` for high-utility pages that need title + primary action.
5. `a-c-statistics-11` and `a-c-statistics-12` for KPI strips that stay compact.
6. `a-c-tables-14` and `a-c-tables-15` for searchable data-heavy ledgers and queues.

Adaptation note: use the anatomy, not the component literally. Keep Silversea's flat operational feel, its Vietnamese labels, and its existing primitives (`PageHeader`, `Breadcrumbs`, `Sidebar`, `ClickableCard`, `Modal`, `Tabs`, `Table`).

### Untitled UI, ranked best fit
1. `sidebar-dual-tier` for a dense admin shell that separates primary and secondary navigation.
2. `filter-bar` for shipment/AR/AP queue controls.
3. `table` for responsive ledger and work-queue presentation.
4. `breadcrumbs` for nested finance and detail pages.
5. `empty-state` for no-data and no-match states.
6. `charts-base` for profitability and KPI visualization.
7. `command-menu` for rapid role-safe navigation and action discovery.

Adaptation note: good source for interaction structure and spacing discipline, but the dependencies are heavier. Use conceptually, not by copy-paste, because Silversea already has its own shell, routing, and design tokens.

### Shadcn
- No live MCP component surface was available in this session, so I did not use exact Shadcn component IDs.
- The closest local equivalent is Silversea's own minimal composition layer: `PageHeader`, `Card`, `Modal`, `Tabs`, `Table`, `Sidebar`, `Topbar`, and `Breadcrumbs`.

## Implemented-vs-Gap Findings

### Implemented
- The app already separates office and customer surfaces.
- The customer portal already has legal-entity scoping and mobile bottom navigation.
- The shipments page already uses a bounded list with desktop table + mobile cards and accessible search/filter controls.
- Dispatch already has its own dense operational layout and queue concepts.
- Finance pages already exist for debt, payables, profit, expenses, and governance.

### Gaps
- `Layout.tsx` shows finance items to `ACCOUNTANT`, but `App.tsx` blocks `ACCOUNTANT` and `MANAGER` from `/dispatch`, `/profit`, `/debt`, and `/payables` via `managerOrAdminOnly` and `adminOnly`.
- `DispatchPage` is visible in nav to `ACCOUNTANT`, but the route itself is not.
- `ProfitPage` is visible in nav to `ACCOUNTANT`, but the route itself is not.
- `DebtListPage` and `PayableListPage` are visible in nav to `MANAGER` and `ACCOUNTANT`, but the route guards do not match.
- `CLERK` has create/docs routes, but not a clerk-owned shipment queue or list.
- `CEO` is a business label, not a live role constant, so any CEO-specific behavior must map onto existing manager/admin policy.
- The current IA still behaves like separate screens, not one lifecycle workspace.

## Trade-off Matrix

| Option | Performance | Complexity | Maintenance | Risk | Fit |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Keep current shells, recompose pages into role-aware lifecycle workspaces | Good | Medium | Good | Low | Best |
| 2. Add a new `/finance/*` subtree and mirror the old top-level routes | Good | High | Fair | Medium | Mixed |
| 3. Split by role into separate parallel shells | Good short-term, poor long-term | High | Poor | High | Worst |

Recommendation: option 1.

Why:
- preserves current code and route contracts,
- avoids another navigation system,
- lets you fix the role mismatch once instead of duplicating it,
- works with the current desktop/mobile layout pattern already proven in shipments, dispatch, and customer portal.

## Limitations
- I did not run a browser session or mutation QA. This is research only.
- I did not inspect every page in the app; I focused on the lifecycle surfaces named in the task.
- I did not pull a live Shadcn MCP component catalog in this session because no callable surface appeared.
- Some pages likely have additional edge-case tests and layout rules beyond the ones sampled here.

## Unresolved Questions
- Should `ACCOUNTANT` get read-only access to `/dispatch` and `/profit`, or should those nav entries be removed from the accountant sidebar entirely?
- Should `CLERK` get a shipment queue/list surface, or stay limited to create/docs entry points?
- Should `CEO` be modeled as a distinct role in policy docs only, or should the app introduce an explicit enum/alias mapping?
- Should `/debt` and `/payables` remain top-level routes, or be grouped under a `/finance` workspace with nested tabs while keeping the old URLs as redirects?
</file>

