<file path="plans/260731-1631-customer-workflow-data-cutover/research/app-data-audit.md">
# App Data Audit for Customer Workflow Cutover

Scope: read-only audit of repo data/control-flow surfaces for a customer-service-finance cutover. No staging connection was made. No product code or data was changed.

## Executive Summary

Ranked recommendation:

1. Preserve identity, access, governance, and audit tables as non-reseeded state. That includes `users`, `userCustomerLinks`, `userShipmentLinks`, `userBusinessUnitLinks`, `auditLogs`, `appSettings`, `roadConfig`, `periodLocks`, and `governanceActions`. These are the control plane, not disposable demo data.
2. Treat customer workflows, shipments, trips, expenses, and portal content as reloadable business data, but only through an FK-aware import/wipe layer that deletes children first and can re-run safely.
3. Preserve financial authorities unless the cutover is explicitly a fresh accounting sandbox. In this repo, `ledger`, `billingDocuments`, `paymentReceipts`, `paymentAllocations`, `paymentRefunds`, and `debtOffsets` are not safe to bulk-wipe casually.

Why this ranking:

- Auth revalidates against current DB state on every request, so identity links are live control data, not just seed fixtures. See [backend/src/middleware/auth.ts](/Users/dev/Documents/projects/silversea/backend/src/middleware/auth.ts#L1-L161).
- The API mounts customer portal, shipment, and finance routes behind role and scope checks, so cutover must preserve access graph integrity. See [backend/src/index.ts](/Users/dev/Documents/projects/silversea/backend/src/index.ts#L172-L244).
- Seed/reset code proves the repo can rebuild a disposable local dataset, but that path is not a staging-safe accounting reset. See [backend/src/seed.ts](/Users/dev/Documents/projects/silversea/backend/src/seed.ts#L19-L260), [backend/src/reset-seed.ts](/Users/dev/Documents/projects/silversea/backend/src/reset-seed.ts).

## Verified Baseline

Local audit counts captured during repo inspection:

- `users`: 3,621
- active `CUSTOMER` users: 228
- `users.customer_id` non-null: 62
- drivers linked to users: 57
- `user_customer_links`: 57
- `user_business_unit_links`: 28
- `user_shipment_links`: 942,358
- `notifications`: 5,687,108
- `customers`: 1,893
- `shipments`: 3,733
- `trips`: 892
- `audit_logs`: 19,624

Implication: preserve account usability, not just row counts. A cutover that recreates users without the same links, roles, and scope relationships will strand the portal and operator workflows.

## Coverage Matrix

| Area | Status | Why it matters | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
