# Customer Source Audit

## Scope

Audit the customer-provided source documents for a plan-ready inventory:
- workflow stages
- workbook fields and report outputs
- formula / calculation surfaces
- master-data entities
- sample-vs-authoritative ambiguity
- contract obligations that affect the app

Sensitive contact, bank, and tax values are intentionally not repeated here.

## Executive Summary

The source set is split into two authority levels:
- Contract documents are the highest-authority source for operational obligations, payment timing, detention rules, confirmation windows, dispute handling, and force majeure.
- Excel files are mostly templates, demo datasets, and reporting surfaces. They are useful for domain modeling and UI/report scope, but not reliable as live master data.

The app needs one canonical workflow centered on shipment planning, execution, invoicing / debit-note generation, and accounting reconciliation. The workbook family is consistent with that flow:
- master data sheets seed customers, vendors, factories, ports, routes, vehicles, and staff
- dispatch sheets capture booking / bill / container / route / date inputs
- debit-note and transport-summary sheets generate invoice-adjacent outputs
- accounting sheets drive profit/loss, debt aging, reminders, and monthly close

The main decision gate is authority selection when sources conflict:
- use the contract text for legal and settlement rules
- use the accepted business-logic Q&A defaults for cross-cutting policy where the contract is silent
- treat workbook examples as templates unless a customer-specific contract or confirmed seed file says otherwise

## Source Matrix

