## Success Criteria

- [ ] A reviewer can point to one authoritative model for shipment, debt instrument, receivable, payable, collection, and treasury.
- [ ] Official invoice handling has one accepted authority: reference-only on current AR, or a separately reviewed one-posting replacement/alias design; no duplicate lifecycle or balance exists.
- [ ] Treasury KPI source is explicit and persisted, or Phase 5 is blocked from exposing cash/bank metrics.
- [ ] Pending pre-cutover money actions, old clients/keys, account watermarks, and rollback cannot produce a ledger-only or double-counted cash event.
- [ ] Customer milestone, delivery, visible content, and acknowledgement have distinct owners and a portal-safe DTO.
- [ ] Dispatcher, salesperson, and financial posting versions have authoritative lifecycle owners before profitability work starts.
- [ ] All planned schema changes are additive with a documented rollback path.
- [ ] Focused shipment and finance regression suites are named before downstream implementation starts.
- [ ] Phase 2 and Phase 3 can begin without revisiting foundational ownership questions.

## Risks

- High likelihood / high impact: accidental second-ledger design. Mitigation: reject any flow that posts receivable state outside current billing + allocation paths.
- Medium likelihood / high impact: invoice/tax requirements push a second document authority. Mitigation: enforce an adapter/snapshot boundary and link back to `billing_documents`.
- High likelihood / medium impact: treasury KPI pressure causes AR/AP inference. Mitigation: make Phase 5 depend on a persisted company-cash source.
- Medium likelihood / medium impact: migration overreach. Mitigation: additive-only schema, generated migration, replay test, rollback notes.

## Security / RBAC

- Keep shipment write reach to `ADMIN`, `MANAGER`, and `CLERK`; keep `ACCOUNTANT` read-only on shipment operations (`backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:194`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:292`, `backend/src/tests/shipment-rbac.test.ts:101`).
