## Overview

Turn the current shipment backbone into the customer-service operating workspace: create and enrich draft shipments, keep dispatch-readiness visible but non-blocking, add an auditable coordination timeline, and expose customer-safe shipment visibility in the portal. This phase explicitly stops before debit-note confirmation flows, AR collections, and profitability dashboards.

## Key Insights

- Shipment already supports draft creation, quick-create, dossier edits, document attach/replace, declarations, container reconcile, dispatch, and post-dispatch review; the right move is to extend this surface, not replace it (`backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:192`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:235`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:419`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:489`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:574`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:610`).
- Dispatch-readiness is already advisory in tests. Missing B/L or containers should warn, not block, while the shipment is still a partial draft (`backend/src/tests/m102-dispatch-readiness.test.ts:77`, `backend/src/tests/m102-dispatch-readiness.test.ts:131`).
- The portal already scopes shipment list/detail by selected customer and prevents customerId overreach in the browser (`backend/src/routes/portal/index.ts:112`, `backend/src/routes/portal/index.ts:172`, `backend/src/routes/portal/index.ts:205`, `frontend/src/pages/portal/PortalPages.test.tsx:76`, `frontend/src/pages/portal/PortalPages.test.tsx:133`).
- Frontend route and nav reach is already differentiated between office staff, customers, and clerks; any new surface must preserve those guards instead of duplicating them (`frontend/src/App.tsx:154`, `frontend/src/App.tsx:160`, `frontend/src/App.tsx:164`, `frontend/src/App.tsx:221`, `frontend/src/App.tsx:291`, `frontend/src/App.tsx:298`, `frontend/src/components/Layout.tsx:54`, `frontend/src/components/Layout.tsx:107`).
- Shipment change requests already persist an internal review loop after dispatch. Customer-service coordination should complement that loop, not bypass it (`backend/src/services/shipment.service.ts:1707`, `backend/src/tests/shipment-routes.test.ts:1733`).
- Portal shipment UI already has a responsive list/detail contract and tests; extend it with timeline / acknowledgement visibility rather than inventing a second customer shipment experience (`frontend/src/pages/portal/PortalShipmentsPage.tsx:67`, `frontend/src/pages/portal/PortalPages.test.tsx:48`, `frontend/src/pages/portal/PortalPages.test.tsx:103`).

## Requirements

- Keep draft shipment creation partial-friendly. Booking can start with incomplete dossier data.
- Keep dispatch-readiness advisory. It may block the UI action by policy later, but it must not silently hard-fail the authoritative dispatch path in this phase.
- Customer communication v1 is a shipment-scoped interaction/update log plus notifications and acknowledgements, not free-form two-way messaging.
- Preserve existing shipment authority for customer, cargo, route, document, declaration, and container data.
- Preserve portal scoping by authenticated customer assignment; never trust browser-provided customer identity alone.
- Preserve role reach: `CLERK` / `MANAGER` / `ADMIN` can operate shipment
  workflow, `ACCOUNTANT` stays read-only on shipment surfaces, and `CUSTOMER`
  stays portal-only. CUS is the existing `CLERK` role; its later cost-check
  reach is a separate narrowly scoped recoverable-cost read/request capability,
  never coarse `financial` access.
- Every new coordination list, count, detail, acknowledgement, or write accepts the authenticated actor and applies `assertClerkCanAccessShipment` (or the equivalent canonical scope assertion) before reading or mutating the target. Write endpoints revalidate scope inside the write transaction and deny out-of-scope IDs as not found.
- Keep CLERK assignment scope live per request. A cached capability may control navigation, but it never replaces the current business-unit plus customer/shipment assignment check.
- Do not create a generic interaction table until the authority matrix names how it composes with `shipmentMilestones`, `customerEmailLogs`, notifications, and portal history. Operational milestone, delivery attempt, and customer acknowledgement remain separate facts.
- Customer-visible events use a server-controlled `CUSTOMER_VISIBLE` classification, immutable content version, legal-entity/customer scope, and an allowlisted display snapshot. Internal diffs, actor metadata, operational notes, contact data, storage keys, and pending-change details never enter the portal DTO.
- Establish attribution ownership needed downstream: the accepted dispatcher is the authenticated actor on a wired handoff acceptance command, with reassignment/supersession history; salesperson ownership is a versioned shipment/customer-account assignment with effective dates, null rules, and authorized reassignment. Generic creator/handler fields are not substitutes.
- Reuse existing UI shell and consult Shadcn, Untitled UI, and Tailkit patterns early, but adapt them to the Silversea design system and responsive constraints.
- Do not redesign debit-note, AR statement, or payment surfaces here. Those land in later phases.

## Architecture / Data Flow

1. Booking enters shipment draft create or quick-create and generates the shipment shell (`backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:192`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:235`).
2. Customer service updates shipment dossier fields, attaches documents, maintains declarations, and reconciles containers on the same shipment authority (`backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:290`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:419`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:489`, `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:574`).
3. The phase composes an internal operational timeline from the accepted shipment event authority and a separate customer-visible delivery/acknowledgement projection. Acknowledgements target an immutable customer-visible event ID/version; they never acknowledge a mutable shipment row or raw internal diff.
4. Dispatch-readiness evaluates missing dossier requirements and surfaces warnings before dispatch, but dispatch still flows through the existing shipment-to-trip command (`backend/src/services/shipment.service.ts:1523`).
5. After dispatch, any operational plan changes continue through shipment change requests, preserving one approval path (`backend/src/routes/shipments.ts:610`, `backend/src/services/shipment.service.ts:1707`).
6. The portal reads shipment list/detail for the selected customer, plus any new safe timeline/acknowledgement projection approved in Phase 1 (`backend/src/routes/portal/index.ts:172`, `backend/src/routes/portal/index.ts:205`).
7. Dispatch handoff acceptance and salesperson assignment record immutable, versioned attribution events that Phase 5 can snapshot; absent historical evidence remains missing attribution.

