## Risks, Security, And RBAC

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Frontend visibility diverges from backend policy | Server capability contract, matrix tests, direct API/URL denial tests |
| Misleading guard name causes accidental permission change | Characterize actual behavior, use semantic guards, preserve verified policy |
| CLERK queue leaks unassigned shipments or counts | Reuse/prove row-scope service across list/detail/related records and no-assignment cases |
| Test runs as admin while claiming CLERK evidence | Remove fallback; exact-role preflight is P0 |
| Portal roles enter the internal shell | Separate shell guards plus route/API denial matrix |
| High-volume UI becomes one long page | Server pagination/windowing, bounded scroll regions, realistic volume QA |
| Finance values wrap/clip or become compact | Full-digit formatter tests at four viewports |
| Frontend flag grants access before backend | Server-owned mode/capabilities; backend independently authorizes |
| Partial rollout mixes incompatible read models | Versioned contracts, shadow parity, source watermarks, cohort activation |
| Backfill corrupts authority data | Additive/idempotent Drizzle writes, dry-run, reconciliation, no direct ledger rewrite |
| Rollback deletes evidence or source data | Disable reads/writes by mode; retain additive rows; no destructive routine rollback |
| Staging QA changes business data | Read-only production-like checks or dedicated authorized fixtures only |
| Screenshots/logs expose credentials or customer data | Sanitized fixture data, no secrets in artifacts, review before handoff |

### Security checks

- Test authentication expiry, role change during session, revoked assignment,
  direct object reference, pagination cursor tampering, search/count side
  channels, export/download endpoints, attachment/document access, and
  optimistic-concurrency conflict.
- Verify audit actor, timestamp, source version, approval/rejection reason, and
  rollback-mode transition for governed actions.
- Keep capability metadata minimal; do not return hidden financial values or
  source counts to unauthorized roles.
- Use test fixtures rather than credentials in commands, screenshots, reports,
  or plan artifacts.

## Migration, Backfill, Rollout, And Rollback

### Migration/backfill checklist

- [ ] Inventory Phase 1-5 migrations and dependency order.
- [ ] Apply forward migrations on empty and production-like databases.
- [ ] Prove the previous application build can read the additive schema and
  classify whether it meets the post-cutover payment compatibility floor; an
  incompatible build must have direct-money writes hard-blocked.
- [ ] Run all backfills in dry-run and record source/target counts.
- [ ] Run bounded active backfill and interrupt/resume it.
- [ ] Rerun to prove idempotency.
- [ ] Reconcile financial totals, source watermarks, and missing-attribution
  classifications.
- [ ] Confirm no raw/direct rewrite of ledger, shipment, billing, receipt, or
  distribution authority.

### Activation gates

- [ ] Previous phases green with reviewer evidence.
- [ ] Zero financial residual.
- [ ] Zero unauthorized successful route/API action.
- [ ] Exact-role fixture preflight green.
- [ ] Zero unclassified orphan or silent attribution fallback.
- [ ] Accepted, explicit missing-attribution rate.
- [ ] Desktop/tablet/mobile P0 matrix green.
- [ ] High-volume performance within agreed threshold.
- [ ] Alerts, owner, observation window, and rollback command ready.

### Rollback triggers

- Any accounting residual or source-watermark regression.
- Any cross-role or cross-customer/business-unit data exposure.
- Any duplicate financial/write event.
- Any migration/backfill corruption signal.
- Sustained report/API latency or failure beyond the agreed threshold.
- A P0 workflow action becomes inaccessible for an authorized role.

### Rollback procedure

1. Stop cohort expansion.
2. Set rollout mode to `SHADOW` or `OFF` through the approved server-owned
   configuration path.
3. Verify new governed writes are stopped or remain safely dual-observed
   according to the phase contract.
4. Verify compatible old reads/routes remain available.
5. Preserve additive schema/snapshot/audit rows.
6. Restore only a build that meets the post-cutover compatibility floor. If no
   such build is available, keep direct-money actions gated while compatible
   read paths remain available.
7. Run role smoke, authority-chain reconciliation, and health checks.
8. Record incident evidence and do not reactivate until the root cause is fixed
   and the full affected gate reruns green.

## Documentation And Handoff

- Create/update an operator-facing workflow document only after route names,
  roles, states, and commands are verified.
- Document:
  - authority chain and explicit non-authorities;
  - role workspace and action matrix;
  - rollout modes and owners;
  - migration/backfill order and status commands;
  - reconciliation definitions;
  - alerts and rollback triggers;
  - known, accepted missing-attribution classifications.
- Update architecture docs only if the capability or reporting composition is a
