# Phase 3: Import and Preservation Tooling

## Overview

Build a dry-run-first, replay-safe importer and cleanup tool that preserves the
complete account control plane while replacing only approved business data.

## Requirements

- Functional: inspect, validate, plan, apply, reconcile, and rollback are
  separate commands/modes.
- Functional: preserve all user IDs, credential hashes, roles, status, deletion
  state, approved access/profile mappings, and user-owned rows.
- Functional: remap or rebuild derived shipment/customer scope to new IDs.
- Non-functional: Drizzle-only application writes, one transaction per cutover
  unit, idempotency, bounded memory, redacted output, and no embedded secrets.

## Architecture

```text
Approved manifest + source files
  -> parser/normalizer
  -> validation staging model
  -> dry-run diff + preservation proof
  -> transactional child-first cleanup/upsert
  -> scope rebuild
  -> reconciliation report
```

Backup/restore is an infrastructure prerequisite outside the transaction.

The parser/normalizer and read-only validator may be developed from Phase 1
outputs before Phase 2 completes. No apply/reconciliation implementation is
accepted until the final Phase 2 schema and blocking finance decisions are
green.

### Preservation Closure

Always preserve unchanged:

- immutable `users` identity/profile, credential, role, status, account-type,
  timestamp, and deletion fields;
- `user_business_unit_links`;
- `business_units` required by those links;
- user-linked `drivers` identity and `drivers.user_id`;
- application settings and RBAC policy inputs.
- governance actions, period locks, and audit evidence.
- `push_subscriptions`, onboarding progress/tasks/events, agent
  conversations/messages/metrics, actor-linked idempotency and audit history,
  notifications, and every other row discovered by the user-FK and
  semantic-user-reference inventory.

Preserve or explicitly remap:

- `users.customer_id` (runtime FK added by migration);
- `user_customer_links`;
- customer master rows required by existing account mappings.
- finance/governance customer anchors and every typed or polymorphic customer
  reference, including ledger/document entities, allocation targets, lock
  scopes, snapshots, and JSON metadata.

Rebuild as derived data:

- `user_shipment_links` for imported/recreated shipment IDs.

Finance, audit, governance, notification, and user-owned history are preserved
by default. A named exclusion is a separate, explicitly approved maintenance
plan; it is never inferred from the business-data cleanup request. Never delete
an unresolved account, customer, or historical entity anchor.

### Mutation and Reference Closure

- Generate an environment-specific, versioned disposition manifest assigning
  every table and exact row predicate to `PRESERVE`, `REMAP`, `REBUILD`, or
  `DELETE`, with expected counts and typed/polymorphic dependents.
- Default deny: the tool refuses to mutate a table or row absent from the
  approved disposition manifest.
- Inventory FK plus semantic references such as `audit_logs.entityId`,
  `notifications.relatedEntityId`, `idempotency_keys.entityId`, onboarding
  metadata, snapshots, JSON identifiers, ledger/document entities, allocation
  targets, and period-lock scopes.
- Prefer stable customer/entity IDs through in-place upsert. Where replacement
  is approved, use a complete transactional old-to-new map, tombstone anchor,
  or immutable archival snapshot so history still resolves to the same logical
  entity.

## Related Code Files

- Create: `backend/src/scripts/customer-cutover.ts`
- Create: `backend/src/services/customer-cutover.service.ts`
- Create: `backend/src/services/customer-cutover-manifest.ts`
- Create: `backend/src/tests/customer-cutover.test.ts`
- Modify: `backend/package.json` for explicit cutover commands
- Read/adapt patterns: `backend/src/seed.ts`, `backend/src/reset-seed.ts`
- Read: `backend/src/db/schema.ts`, migrations, `Makefile`,
  `deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml`
- Create: `docs/customer-data-cutover-runbook.md` only when commands/operations
  are stable.

## Implementation Steps

1. Define a versioned manifest schema with source hashes, classifications,
   natural keys, transforms, contract mapping, disposition rules, environment
   identity, schema/tool build, and approval metadata.
2. Parse workbooks without changing them; normalize dates, numbers, tax IDs,
   plates, container IDs, and Vietnamese text deterministically.
3. Validate duplicates, missing natural keys, invalid references, formula
   outputs, and forbidden sample rows before opening a write transaction.
4. Capture a DB-side preservation snapshot containing counts and safe digests;
   do not export password hashes or sensitive row content.
5. Build a complete user-FK, entity-FK, polymorphic, JSON, snapshot, and
   semantic-reference graph from the live database and code; refuse to apply if
   it differs from the approved/tested inventory.
6. Upsert approved master data by stable natural key while retaining stable IDs
   wherever preserved history refers to them; otherwise produce and apply a
   complete transactional old-to-new ID map.
7. Load the environment-specific disposition manifest and delete only exact
   `DELETE` predicates in child-first order after expected-count and dependency
   checks. Refuse any unlisted table/row. Never use blanket
   `TRUNCATE ... CASCADE`.
   Never reset sequences or reuse deleted entity IDs, because preserved audit
   and notification rows may retain historical entity references.
8. Import transactions only if Phase 1 classified them authoritative; otherwise
   import no historical amounts.
9. Remap `users.customer_id`, `user_customer_links`, and every typed or
   polymorphic customer reference transactionally; retain required legacy
   customer/entity anchors when no approved mapping exists.
10. Preserve linked driver rows and update approved master attributes without
    changing `user_id`.
11. Rebuild `user_shipment_links` from approved assignment/customer/business
    rules and prove no orphan or cross-customer links.
12. Record immutable cutover-run state uniquely keyed by environment identity,
    authority/disposition manifest hashes, source hashes, schema migration, and
    importer commit/build digest, plus the redacted reconciliation and ID-map
    references.
13. Reconcile counts, natural keys, financial equations, preservation digests,
    and zero stale semantic references before commit; rollback on any
    unexplained delta.
14. On an identical replay, return the committed reconciliation result without
    deleting or importing again; prove unchanged PKs, links, timestamps,
    sequences, and row counts.
15. If a named user-owned dataset is separately approved for archival/purge,
    execute it as a distinct resumable, checkpointed maintenance plan after the
    cutover; never bundle it into this request.

## Success Criteria

- [ ] Dry-run performs zero writes and outputs an actionable redacted diff.
- [ ] Tool refuses unapproved rows, unknown contracts, or unmapped accounts.
- [ ] All preserved user columns are byte-for-byte unchanged after apply.
- [ ] No dangling or stale FK, typed, polymorphic, JSON, snapshot, audit,
      notification, idempotency, governance, billing, allocation, or ledger
      reference remains.
- [ ] Re-running preserves PKs, timestamps, sequences, links, and counts and
      returns the committed reconciliation without new mutations.
- [ ] Failure at any import step rolls back the complete cutover unit.
- [ ] Logs contain no password hash, token, personal contact, or bank value.

## Test Scenario Matrix

| Scenario | Expected |
|---|---|
| Duplicate tax ID/plate/container | fail before write |
| Unmapped CUSTOMER account | block delete/import |
| Deleted/locked account | preserved exactly |
| Imported shipment IDs change | scope rebuilt and all historical references resolve |
| Mid-transaction error | zero committed business changes |
| Finance-reset flag absent | finance tables untouched |
| Unlisted disposition row | reject before write |
| Second apply | same committed result; no PK/timestamp/sequence/count change |

## Risk Assessment

Very high blast radius. The tool must default to inspect/dry-run, require an
explicit environment and manifest fingerprint for apply, and reject production.
</file>

