# Phase 3: Fulfillment tasks and dispatch

## Context Links

- [Plan](./plan.md)
- `backend/src/services/dispatch-handoff.service.ts`
- `backend/src/services/shipment.service.ts`
- `backend/src/services/trip-mutations.service.ts`
- `backend/src/services/trip-status-machine.service.ts`
- `frontend/src/pages/DispatchPage.tsx`
- `frontend/src/features/dispatch/`

## Overview

Connect the existing shipment handoff to a bounded dispatcher queue. Decompose FCL by container and LCL as one unit, create one linked trip per fulfillment, explicitly assign carrier/plate/driver, and issue an auditable order with in-app/Web Push notification.

## Key Insights

- Handoff CAS exists but has no frontend; `markSeen` is not product-wired.
- Current shipment dispatch creates one trip and snapshots every container.
- Truck launch uses an advisory lock, but planning needs date/time overlap checks for truck, trailer, and driver before issue.
- `/dispatch` currently loads and renders all CREATED/IN_TRANSIT pages; it must use server pagination and bounded panes.

## Requirements

### Functional

- Accepted handoff creates/reconciles deterministic fulfillment units, never duplicates them.
- Queue rows show customer, site, bill/booking, declaration, container/LCL summary, cutoff, closing/return time, urgency, dispatch notes, and site-rule snapshot.
- Dispatcher selects carrier, truck/plate, trailer when required, and driver explicitly; any assigned-driver suggestion remains editable and is revalidated server-side.
- Each order has `plannedStartAt` and `plannedEndAt`. End time comes from an approved route service duration and must be explicitly confirmed when the route has no duration; intervals are half-open `[start, end)`.
- Planned interval conflicts, invalid driver-user binding, inactive resources, capacity mismatch, and stale versions block issue with useful errors.
- Issuing an order creates/updates the linked CREATED trip, records dispatcher attribution, marks fulfillment dispatched, and emits one durable `TRIP_DISPATCHED` notification with driver deep link.
- Reassignment uses `expectedVersion`, preserves history, and never silently moves an active trip.

### Non-functional

- Server-side filters, sort, and pagination before hydration. Task page size is capped at 50, the fleet pane at 100, and the UI renders no more than the loaded page (50 task rows/cards and 55 seeded vehicles in acceptance QA).
- With the 500-task/55-vehicle fixture, the primary queue request uses at most 8 database statements and its controlled warm integration check completes within 1 second; record both query count and elapsed time.
- Desktop has independently scrollable queue/fleet/detail regions; tablet/mobile uses bounded task/fleet tabs.
- No route optimizer, continuous tracking, or external messaging channel.

## Architecture

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant C as CUS shipment
  participant H as Dispatch handoff
  participant F as Fulfillment service
  participant D as Dispatcher
  participant T as Trip authority
  participant N as Notification bus
  C->>H: handoff with expectedVersion
  D->>H: seen and accept
  H->>F: decompose idempotently
  D->>F: assign explicit resources
  F->>T: create/update linked CREATED trip
  T->>N: TRIP_DISPATCHED
```

## File Inventory

| Action | File | Purpose | Test impact |
|---|---|---|---|
| Modify | `backend/src/services/dispatch-handoff.service.ts` | Wire seen/accept/decompose | Handoff CAS |
| Modify | `backend/src/routes/shipments.ts` | Handoff/fulfillment APIs | Shipment RBAC |
| Modify | `backend/src/services/shipment.service.ts` | Replace all-container single-trip dispatch | Shipment tests |
| Modify | `backend/src/services/trip-mutations.service.ts` | Fulfillment-scoped trip creation | Trip/finance |
| Create | `backend/src/services/dispatch-planning.service.ts` | Queue, assignment, conflicts, issue | New tests |
| Modify | `backend/src/services/notification.service.ts` | Deep links and exact recipients | Notification tests |
| Modify | `shared/src/constants/index.ts` | Push rule for actionable dispatch | Shared tests |
| Modify | `frontend/src/pages/DispatchPage.tsx` | Handoff/task workbench | Frontend |
| Modify | `frontend/src/features/dispatch/` | Paginated data, task cards, assignment | Frontend |
| Modify | `frontend/src/lib/notificationClient.ts` | Driver deep-link behavior | Frontend |
| Create | `backend/src/tests/dispatch-fulfillment.test.ts` | Decomposition/assignment/races | Backend |

## Exact Interface Checklist

- [x] Product-wire `markSeen()` with actor ownership and expected version.
- [x] `listDispatchFulfillments({ cursor/page, limit, q, status, urgency, date })` **(proposed)** queries in DB.
- [x] `assignFulfillmentResources(..., expectedVersion, idempotencyKey)` **(proposed)** validates explicit carrier/truck/driver/trailer.
- [x] `issueDispatchOrder(..., expectedVersion, idempotencyKey)` **(proposed)** atomically links trip and notification intent.
- [x] Assignment/issue acquires sorted transaction-scoped PostgreSQL advisory locks for driver, truck, and trailer IDs, then rechecks half-open interval overlaps before commit; locks cover distinct fulfillments, not only one handoff version.
- [x] Reuse `normalizeSearchText` for Vietnamese client-side fleet search only; server queue search remains authoritative.
- [x] Preserve `transitionTripStatus()` and truck advisory lock for actual launch.

## Implementation Steps

1. Add tests proving current one-trip behavior, then change decomposition to FCL-per-container/LCL-one-task.
2. Wire handoff inbox, seen/accept/reject, conflict reload, and shipment deep links.
3. Implement paginated dispatch query and independent fleet/task loading states.
4. Implement explicit assignment, availability/capacity validation, CAS, and reassignment history.
5. Issue order atomically and publish durable notification after commit; make Web Push best-effort and replay-safe.
6. Rebuild `/dispatch` as bounded desktop panes and responsive task/fleet tabs using existing design primitives.

## Test Scenario Matrix

| Priority | Scenario | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| P0 | Four-container FCL handoff | Four tasks and four independently assignable trips |
| P0 | LCL handoff | One task/trip; split endpoint unavailable |
| P0 | Two dispatchers accept/assign | First valid version wins; loser reloads |
| P0 | Truck or driver overlaps planned time | Issue blocked |
| P0 | Two different fulfillments concurrently claim the same truck, trailer, or driver interval | Exactly one commits for each resource; the loser receives conflict |
| P0 | One interval ends when another starts | Both allowed under `[start, end)` semantics |
| P0 | Same issue request replayed | Same order/notification, no duplicate trip |
| P1 | Push unavailable | Order persists; in-app notification remains; no rollback |
| P1 | 500 tasks/55 vehicles | Page cap 50/100, no more than loaded nodes rendered, accurate total, <=8 DB statements, controlled warm response <1s |
| P1 | ACCOUNTANT direct issue call | 403 |

## QA and Regression Gate

- Focused handoff/decomposition/notification/dispatch UI tests.
- Full backend/frontend/shared/E2E gates due public flow and RBAC changes.
- Manual responsive/keyboard checks at 1440, 1024, 768, 390, and 320px with realistic Vietnamese content.

## Success Criteria

- [x] Handoff and dispatch workbench form one connected workflow.
- [x] Resource assignment is explicit, validated, versioned, and auditable.
- [x] Queue performance is bounded at the approved volume.
- [x] Driver receives exactly one actionable App/Web Push order with correct deep link.
- [x] No forbidden messaging or tracking integration exists.

## Risks, Security, and Rollback

Main risks are lost updates, double dispatch, schedule collisions, and notification-before-commit. Use row locks, unique keys, transactional outbox/after-commit delivery, expected versions, and server-side role checks. Unreleased schema/UI can be forward-corrected or reset in dev/staging.

## Next Steps

Phase 4 executes issued tasks through the driver portal.
