# Accountant Workflow Codebase Gap Map

## Executive Summary

The accountant workbook does not exist as a single product surface yet. What exists today is a distributed set of finance authorities: billing documents and Debit Note issuance, payment receipt/allocation/refund flows, debt-offset governance, ledger/statements, P&L/reporting, reminder/email delivery, and role-gated navigation. The core backend behavior is real and test-covered, but it is split across many services and pages instead of being composed into one accountant-centered workbook.

The codebase is therefore closer to an authority platform than a workbook. That is good for correctness. The existing `/debt`, `/payables`, `/finance`, and `/profit` destinations already form the accountant workspace; the gap is workbook-specific projection and presentation, not a new route. The main implementation risk is duplicated authority and repeated period/currency logic drifting apart.

## Product decision update

Adversarial review initially recommended keeping `/debt` as home while the route
decision was unresolved. The user has now explicitly selected `/accounting` as
the dedicated `Kế toán` home. It must be a thin composition shell rather than a
new finance authority. Manual reminder send remains deferred because only a
scheduler contract exists. Upstream finance/dispatch plans own shared billing,
AR/AP, and reporting authorities; this plan owns the accountant composition.

## Research Methodology

- Sources consulted: approved PRD Q&A, both active workflow plans, shared schema/navigation files, backend route/service files, frontend finance surfaces, and test files.
- Date range of materials: current on-disk code and project plan documents inspected on 2026-08-01.
- Key search terms: `Debit Note`, `billing documents`, `payment receipts`, `refund`, `debt offsets`, `ledger`, `P&L`, `dashboard widgets`, `receivable reminder`, `email settings`, `Casbin`, `pageCatalog`, `accountant`.
- Independent evidence used for key claims: plan docs, live code, and tests. Where a behavior was only mentioned in prior rollout memory, I marked it as background context, not authority.

## Inventory By Workbook Family

